<header><p class="enc-kicker">Institution</p><h1 class="enc-title">Sande society</h1><p class="enc-meta">Also known as Sande, Bondo</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">The Sande society is a women's initiation society of Liberia, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sierra-leone">Sierra Leone</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/guinea">Guinea</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ivory-coast">Côte d'Ivoire</a>, the female counterpart of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/poro">Poro</a>. It prepares girls for adulthood and upholds moral and social order, and its masks, worn by women, are among the few in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/africa">Africa</a> danced by women.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/sande-society.jpg" alt="Sande society" width="320" height="437" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: photo: C. H. Firmin. · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Bundu_girls_whitened.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>The Vai script, an indigenous syllabary devised in the 1830s, and the Poro and <b>Sande</b> societies of the interior reflect the rich cultures of the indigenous peoples.</blockquote><a href="/empire/liberia">Liberia · Socioeconomy</a></li></ul></section>
